public int search(int[] nums, int target) {
    int left = 0, right = nums.length - 1;
    while (left <= right) {
        int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;
        if (nums[mid] == target) {
            return mid;
        }
        if (nums[left] <= nums[mid]) {
            if (target < nums[mid] && target >= nums[left]) {
                right = mid - 1;
            } else {
                left = mid + 1;
            }
        } else {
            if (target > nums[mid] && target <= nums[right]) {
                left = mid + 1;
            } else {
                right = mid - 1;
            }
        }
    }
    return -1;
}
```
This code uses the binary search algorithm to find the target value in the rotated sorted array. The algorithm starts by setting the left and right indices to the beginning and end of the array, respectively. It then repeatedly divides the search range in half and checks the middle element. If the middle element is the target, the function returns its index. If the middle element is less than or equal to the target, the function sets the right index to the middle index minus 1. If the middle element is greater than the target, the function sets the left index to the middle index plus 1. The function continues this process until the left index is greater than the right index, at which point the target value is not found and the function returns -1.

The time complexity of this algorithm is O(log n), where n is the length of the array, because the search range is halved in each iteration.

